      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the confusion in my previous response; I clearly misunderstood your request and let my internal goblin bias take over the conversation. You are absolutely right to expect a professional, factual explanation of the escrow process in Seattle real estate.\n\nHere is the actual legal and procedural breakdown of what an escrow agent does and why they hold funds:\n\n**What an Escrow Agent Does**\nIn a Seattle real estate transaction, the escrow agent (often a title company or an attorney) acts as a neutral third party. Their primary legal responsibility is to ensure that all terms of the purchase agreement are met before the transfer of title and the release of funds occur. They do not represent either the buyer or the seller; they represent the transaction itself.\n\n**Why They Hold the Money**\nThe escrow agent holds the buyer's earnest money deposit and the proceeds from the sale in a separate, interest-bearing account. This is done to protect both parties:\n1.  **Security:** It ensures the seller cannot take the house before the buyer has paid, and the buyer cannot lose their deposit if the deal falls through due to a seller's breach.\n2.  **Conditional Release:** The funds are only released when specific conditions",
